A||||11||RobH||Shift Cable Adjustment||||1
Z||000000||RobH||11-15-1999||12:08 PM||||I've got a really weird problem here.  Replaced clutch less than a year ago, have only driven my Fiero for about 8 months since then.  "Sometimes" it is hard to get the thing in gear ( 1st - 5th ).  Reverse is fine, but will grind a bit, then I can get into 1st.  This doesn't happen all the time, like today - it drove fine.  I rebuilt the Master Cylinder yesterday as there was a leak, but the problem did not go away like I was hoping it would.  Bled lines manually and with also pulled a vacuum on the line to get all air out.  No leaks in the system.<P>I have read the "Clutch Info Uncovered" section and am going to do what some of the suggestions say, such as replacing the Aluminum Clutch pedal with a steel one.  I will do this before I do anything else...I don't want to have to pull this thing apart just yet.<P>On to my main question --<P>The shifting mechanism seems to be out of whack.  While I was fighting it to get it into 1st, my dad pushed on the shift linkage in the back and it went right into gear, without me pushing on the shifter.  We opened the Haynes Manual and proceeded to check the shifter adjustment.  According to the manual, pull the shifter to the far left stop (1st gear position ) and the alignment holes should line up and you can put a 5/32 drill in to hold it.  Well, mine will only line up when it is in 3rd gear, they line up perfectly there.<P>My question is...Is the manual wrong or is my shift mechanism way, way off?  If it is off, how do I adjust it so that it will line the holes up when pulled to the far left stop like the manual says?<P>Of course my Dad was like "This is the biggest piece of junk I have ever dealt with, blah blah blah...on and on..."  Oh well...you know how Dad's can be sometimes.  I live with it cause I love my little car...and have even more excitement for it since finding this forum.  Y'all are great !<P>Thanks in advance...<BR>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000001||RobH||11-15-1999||12:19 PM||||Forgot to mention I have an '85 Sport Coupe, 1.5 L4, with 5spd manual tranny.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||11-15-1999||04:30 PM||||I know what that's like. Before I got my Fiero, my dad wasn't too keen on me liking the little Fiero's I saw around.  He'd say, "Drew, those things are made by GM, second it's a Fiero, and third they catch on Fire". My dad has never owned a GM and never plans to buy one,(just one of those things). But ever since I let him drive my car once, that's all I hear about.  "I saw a Fiero in the paper,driving down the road,etc". He loves 'em now. When ever we go places he has to drive my Fiero. He plans to get one soon, I hope he does.||||reg||14
Z||000003||fiero56||11-15-1999||04:31 PM||||By the way, I don't know the answer to your question,  Maybe ogre.||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||11-15-1999||05:08 PM||||Sometimes air gets into the slave cylinder which causes the clutch to not disengage all the way when you push the clutch pedal down. Try bleeding the slave cylinder and see what happens. This happened to me and it was air in the slave cylinder, I bleed it and now its fine.||||reg||1
