Z||000009||WKDFIRO||11-17-1999||04:15 AM||||I had some problems with my shifting and this is how I found the problem:<BR>1) Make sure your cables aren't binding up. easy to disconnect and move them by hand if you can't theres your problem.<BR>2) Grab the shift arm on the tranny, by pushing it up and down you should be able to shift into the gears by hand. This is where if found that my first gear had gone out.<BR>3)The Haynes method is perfect, follow it and forget it. If you are lined up like it says than thats not your problem.<P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000010||VISCERAL||11-17-1999||03:26 PM||||The service manual says to loosen the select<BR>nut in the back, place the shifter into third, then place alignment pin, tighten select cable nut w/o disturbing the linkage. That's it, no matter what haynes says, it's wrong. Shifted better afterwards with the service manual method than with the haynes. good luck||||reg||4
Z||000011||Patrick||11-17-1999||04:13 PM||||<BR>It's been almost three years since I did it, so pardon my hazy memory, but I seem to recall that there WAS some problem with the Haynes method of adjusting the shifter linkage. Is there a chance that there is some confusion between the adjustment needed for a <B>4-speed</B> as opposed to a <B>5-speed</B>?||||reg||5
